INT. ST. AGNES LYING-IN HOSPITAL - CONSULTING ROOM - DAY

London, 1923. Rain falls on the high windows without stopping. The gas lamp on the wall makes a small yellow light. The room smells of carbolic soap and old wood.

Margaret sits on a hard chair. Her husband Edmund stands near the window and does not look at her. The doctor sits behind a big desk with many papers.

DR. WHITCOMBE
(he opens a file and reads slowly)
Mrs. Ashworth, you have been married six years. There is no child. This is the third time you come to me.

MARGARET
(her hands are tight in her lap)
Yes, doctor. We hope you have some new idea.

The rain becomes stronger. A cart passes in the street below and its wheels make a loud noise on the stones. In the corridor a clock strikes the half hour.

EDMUND
(he turns from the window, his voice is cold)
It is not an idea we need. It is an answer. My family asks me every Sunday. Every Sunday.

DR. WHITCOMBE
(he closes the file)
Mr. Ashworth, I must say it plainly. The examination shows no fault in your wife.

A long quiet. The lamp makes a small sound. Outside, a bell rings far away, and the grey light on the wall becomes darker.

MARGARET
(she looks up at him for the first time)
Then the fault is not mine.

EDMUND
(he steps forward, his hand on the desk)
You say this to me? In front of her?

DR. WHITCOMBE
(he stands, calm but not soft)
I say what is true. Please sit down.

Edmund does not sit. He walks to the door, he stops, he comes back. His face is red, and the rain is loud now, and the room feels very small.

The door opens without a knock. SISTER BRIGID, a tall nurse in a starched white veil, comes in with a metal tray. She puts it down and she stands very straight beside the desk.

SISTER BRIGID
(her voice is quiet but it carries)
Doctor, forgive me. The results you asked for from the laboratory. Both sets.

DR. WHITCOMBE
(he takes the paper and reads it, and his face changes)
Thank you, Sister. Stay, please.

EDMUND
(he reaches for the paper)
Give me that.

SISTER BRIGID
(she puts her hand flat on the tray)
Sir, it is not yours to take.

MARGARET
(very softly)
Edmund. Please. Sit.

EDMUND
(he turns on her, loud)
Do not tell me what to do! Six years I wait. Six years my mother looks at you like you are a broken clock!

Thunder, low and long, across the roofs of the city. The lamp shakes. The window glass hums.

DR. WHITCOMBE
(he holds up the paper)
The laboratory is certain, Mr. Ashworth. The difficulty is with you. Not with her. It was never with her.

EDMUND
(he stops, he cannot speak, then he laughs one time)
That is a lie. That is a doctor's lie to make a woman feel better.

MARGARET
(she stands, her chair scrapes the floor)
You never once asked if it was you. Not once. You looked at me. Always at me.

EDMUND
(he knocks the tray from the desk, the instruments fall and ring on the floor)
Enough!

SISTER BRIGID
(she does not move back, her chin lifts)
You will leave this room now, sir. Or I will call the porters.

The rain falls on. A long moment. Edmund looks at the three of them, at the paper, at the wet window, and he takes his hat from the chair and he goes out. The door shakes in its frame.

MARGARET
(she sits down slowly, and begins to breathe again)
He will not come home tonight.

DR. WHITCOMBE
(gently)
Then you will stay here, Mrs. Ashworth. Sister Brigid will make you tea.

SISTER BRIGID
(she bends and picks up the instruments one by one)
Strong tea. With sugar.

The clock in the corridor strikes the hour. The rain is soft again. The lamp is steady.
